(agent_runner.py:1054-1080). So if the gate never submits, the task is graded on the workspace diff, the same as v009's no-submit path.
Termination after a gate SUBMIT: the root's AgentTool call has skip_summarization=true, so its function-response event is final
(google/adk/events/event.py:295) and the root LLM flow stops without another model call (google/adk/flows/llm_flows/base_llm_flow.py:306).
runner.run_async ends, and the runner breaks on the flag (agent_runner.py:920). There are no extra root turns.
After a gate EDIT_FIRST the root run also ends (same skip_summarization). patch_submitted is False, so the harness sends a continuation nudge
(agent_runner.py:961-1010). The nudge counter resets because the turn had a tool call (l.961-962), so bounces cannot use up the 3 nudges.
The root sees the gate's NOT SUBMITTED text as the tool response, then the nudge.
(c) NO name keying, so the gate keeps the name `submit_patch`. Termination keys only on `context.patch_submitted` (agent_runner.py:859,
920). No other code in swegemma or adk_eval_core references the name: the only hits outside tools are prompt/nudge strings and log text
(agent_runner.py:411, 993-1003, 1082-1096). adk_submission has no reference to `submit_patch` at all (grep: 0 hits). handle_adk_event
uses function-call names only for the activity preview (context.py:231-260). The initial prompt's `_has_tool` checks declared names
(agent_runner.py:192-240, 336-337) and finds `submit_patch` (the AgentTool name), so the prompt lines are unchanged. An EDIT_FIRST reply
cannot end the session, because the flag is not set.
(d) YES, gate work counts against the caps. Tool calls: the inner `run_command` is @budget_gated (execution.py:20). That means
+1 against the 40-call cap per gate invocation, through the shared ctx.check_budget (context.py:468-552). The inner `submit_patch` is
