(d) YES, gate work counts against the caps. Tool calls: the inner `run_command` is @budget_gated (execution.py:20). That means
+1 against the 40-call cap per gate invocation, through the shared ctx.check_budget (context.py:468-552). The inner `submit_patch` is
count_tool_call=False (execution.py:76), and the root AgentTool call itself is not budget-gated. Turns: AgentTool propagates the parent
plugins (agent_tool.py:265-277, include_plugins default True at l.149; adk_submission constructs it with defaults, resolvers/tools.py:116-117).
EventDisplayPlugin.on_event_callback (adk_eval_core/plugins/display_plugin.py:84-95) calls the runner's _on_event (agent_runner.py:660-665),
which calls trace.record_event + context.handle_adk_event, and that function counts each model-authored, non-pure-function-response
event as an LLM call (context.py:285-299; dedup by event id l.217-222). The analyzer sub-agent is counted the same way in arm 1.
Per gate invocation: EDIT_FIRST = 2 turns (run_command call, reply) + 1 tool call. SUBMIT = 3 turns (run_command call, submit_patch
call, reply) + 1 tool call. WORST CASE per task vs the builtin (2 EDIT_FIRST rounds then a submit): +3 tool calls (of 40), +7 turns (of 50).
The child runner is not turn-capped itself, so a gate started at turn 49-50 can overrun by <=3 turns. The outer loop then breaks
(agent_runner.py:947), and the patch is either already submitted or collected by the fallback. Near the call cap: the gate's run_command returns
BudgetExceeded, the gate takes step 4 and submits (submit_patch is not call-gated). If the TIME cap has passed, submit_patch returns
TimeoutExceeded and the workspace fallback applies (b).
VERDICT: (a) YES, so the gate design is alive; no rename. One remaining assumption: the scorer runs the same swegemma 0.2.11. The smoke tests the Kaggle kernel copy.

